{
  "translation": "Good morning, Mr. Popa. How are you feeling now? Good morning. Not very well, Doctor. The cough hasn't gone away, and for three days I've had a fever, thirty-eight and something in the evening. Do you cough with phlegm? Yes, mostly in the morning, it's green. And I get very tired, barely making it to the second floor. Do you still smoke? I've reduced to ten cigarettes a day, but I haven't quit completely. Let me listen to your lungs. Breathe deeply through your mouth. Again. It hurts a bit on the right side when I breathe deeply. I hear some crackles at the base of the right lung. I want a chest X-ray today or at the latest tomorrow. Where do I get it done? At the county hospital, in radiology. I'll give you the referral now and you call to schedule an appointment. Okay. And what about the fever? I'll prescribe amoxicillin with clavulanic acid, one thousand milligrams, twice a day, for seven days. For the fever, take paracetamol, no more than four tablets a day. Can I go to work? I work on a construction site. No. I'll give you a medical leave of absence for five days, up to Tuesday inclusive. Rest and drink plenty of fluids. I need a certificate for my employer. I'll issue the medical leave certificate now, you take it to human resources. Thank you. And the tests? Do a complete blood count and C-reactive protein, tomorrow morning, on an empty stomach. I'll do them tomorrow. When should I come back? I'll be waiting on Tuesday, November tenth, at half past ten, with the X-ray and the tests. If you have difficulty breathing or the fever goes above thirty-nine, come immediately or call the emergency department. I understand. And regarding smoking? I recommend the smoking cessation program at the clinic. I'll put you on the list and the assistant, Mrs. Elena, will call you next week. Okay, I'll try. Thank you, Doctor."
}
